---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Download, ArrowUpRight, Clock, Flame, Snowflake } from '@lucide/astro';
import { env } from 'cloudflare:workers';
import { getData } from '../lib/data';
import DataNotice from '../components/DataNotice.astro';
import { historicalView } from '../lib/historical';
import historicalData from '../data/historical-analogs.json';

const { snapshot, stale } = await getData(env);
const view = historicalView(snapshot, stale);
const currentYear = view.year ?? 'Unavailable';
const benchmarks = historicalData.benchmarks.map(b => ({
  ...b,
  extreme: b.weeks.reduce((a, p) => Math.abs(p.anomaly) > Math.abs(a.anomaly) ? p : a),
}));
const signed = (value: number) => (value > 0 ? '+' : '') + value.toFixed(1) + '°C';
const payload = JSON.stringify({ current: view.current, currentYear, benchmarks }).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<Layout title="Historical ENSO Comparison: 1997, 2015, 2020 & 2023" description={`Compare ${currentYear} weekly Niño 3.4 anomalies with 1997, 2015, 2020 and 2023 using NOAA CPC data. See dates and historical-analog limits.`} active="historical">
  <div class="page-shell subpage">
    <div class="page-intro"><div>
      <span class="eyebrow">HISTORICAL COMPARISON · WEEKLY NIÑO 3.4</span>
      <h1>Historical ENSO comparison.<br /><span>{currentYear} against four benchmark years.</span></h1>
      <p>Compare weekly sea-surface temperature departures in the same NOAA CPC series and 1991–2020 baseline. Each line covers one calendar year, not a complete ENSO episode.</p>
    </div></div>

    <DataNotice stale={view.stale} />
    <p class="figure-note">Current curve: latest validated week {view.latestDate ?? 'unavailable'}. Source checked {view.checkedAt}; last successful retrieval {view.retrievedAt}. The displayed year follows the latest observation, including across January.</p>

    <section class="trend-section analog-chart-section" aria-labelledby="analog-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">CALENDAR-YEAR TRAJECTORIES</span><h2 id="analog-heading">Niño 3.4 weekly anomaly progression</h2></div>
        <button class="icon-button download-analog-data" title="Download official historical analog data (CSV)" aria-label="Download official historical analog data as CSV"><Download size={17} /></button>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-toolbar analog-toolbar"><div class="analog-legend-pills">
        <span class="analog-current-pill"><i class="legend-dot current-glow"></i>{currentYear} (Current)</span>
        {benchmarks.map(b => (
          <button class="analog-toggle-pill active" data-analog-toggle={b.id} aria-pressed="true" style={`--pill-color: ${b.color};`}>
            <i class="legend-dot" style={`background-color: ${b.color};`}></i>{b.shortName}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div></div>
      <div class="line-chart-wrap analog-canvas-wrap"><canvas id="analog-chart" role="img" aria-label={`Interactive multi-line chart comparing ${currentYear} weekly Niño 3.4 anomalies against benchmark years 1997, 2015, 2020, and 2023`}></canvas></div>
      <script id="analog-data" type="application/json" set:html={payload} />
      <div class="chart-footer"><span>NOAA CPC WEEKLY DATA · 1991–2020 BASELINE</span><a href="/methodology/#indices">Niño 3.4 method <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a></div>
      <p class="figure-note">Weeks are numbered from January 1 in seven-day bins, including week 53 where present; month labels are approximate. Dates identify the center of each observation week. Similar curves do not predict the same regional weather.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section" aria-labelledby="same-season-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">FIXED SEASONAL SNAPSHOT · CHECKED NOVEMBER 19, 2026</span>
      <h2 id="same-season-heading">How does ASO 2026 compare with earlier El Niño years?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">Compare August–October (ASO) in each year using CPC's ERSSTv6 Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI), with the same 1991–2020 reference. This fixed seasonal example is separate from the live weekly chart above.</p>
      <div class="table-container" role="region" aria-label="August–October RONI comparison" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table seasonal-example">
        <caption>Selected ASO RONI values, as checked November 19, 2026</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">ASO RONI</th><th scope="col">Reading</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">1997</th><td>+2.1°C</td><td>Selected historical season</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">2015</th><td>+1.8°C</td><td>Selected historical season</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">2023</th><td>+1.0°C</td><td>Selected historical season</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">2026</th><td>+2.0°C</td><td>Recent value; subject to revision</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="body-copy">In this snapshot, ASO 2026 is 0.1°C below ASO 1997 and 0.2°C above ASO 2015. These are rounded source values in a selected four-year sample. They do not establish an all-time ranking, a full-event peak or the eventual strength of the 2026–27 episode. Similar seasonal ocean indices also do not imply identical winter rainfall or snow.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Source: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/">CPC RONI table</a>; <a href="/references/worked-examples-2026-11-19.txt">preserved dated values and method</a>. Recent RONI values may revise for up to two months. See the <a href="/methodology/#index-worked-example-heading">same-season ONI/RONI worked example</a> before comparing a conventional index with this relative index.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section" aria-labelledby="enso-frequency-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">RECURRENCE AND EVENT YEARS · CHECKED NOVEMBER 21, 2026</span>
      <h2 id="enso-frequency-heading">How often do El Niño and La Niña happen?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">NOAA describes El Niño and La Niña events as occurring every two to seven years on average, without a regular schedule. That broad recurrence interval differs from an episode's duration, typically nine to twelve months but sometimes years. It cannot tell you the date of the next El Niño or imply that the two phases alternate every year. For the next few seasons, use the <a href="/forecast/">dated CPC phase probabilities</a>.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Source: <a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/ninonina.html">NOAA National Ocean Service: What are El Niño and La Niña?</a>, updated September 23, 2026; checked November 21. <a href="/forecast/when-will-it-end/#double-dip-heading">Why some episodes span multiple winters</a>.</p>
      <h3 id="event-years-heading">How to read an El Niño or La Niña years list</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">A calendar year, a winter pair such as 1997–98, and a continuous run of seasonal values describe different time windows. A year can contain both warm and cold seasons. The weekly curves above are selected calendar-year comparisons; they are not a complete list of historical episodes.</p>
      <div class="table-container" role="region" aria-label="1998 RONI transition example" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table seasonal-example">
        <caption>Three selected seasons in CPC's 1998 RONI row</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Season label</th><th scope="col">Three-month RONI</th><th scope="col">Reading</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">DJF 1998</th><td>+2.1°C</td><td>Warm seasonal value</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">AMJ 1998</th><td>+0.1°C</td><td>Between the ±0.5°C thresholds</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">JJA 1998</th><td>−1.0°C</td><td>Cold seasonal value</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="body-copy">These selected values illustrate the transition within the table's 1998 row; each value alone does not define an episode. CPC colors historical episodes when the ±0.5°C threshold is met for at least five consecutive overlapping three-month seasons. Adjacent seasons share two months, so five seasons are not five independent observations or five individual months. This retrospective convention is separate from the <a href="/faq/#alerts-explained">operational Advisory criteria</a>.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">For the full El Niño and La Niña history since 1950, use the <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/">official CPC seasonal RONI table</a> and follow the consecutive colored seasons across year boundaries. Identify the index and baseline before comparing another list: ONI and RONI can give different values and episode boundaries. A historical list is context, not a countdown to the next event.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Example: CPC ERSSTv6 RONI, 1991–2020 base period; checked November 21, 2026. DJF = December–February, AMJ = April–June, JJA = June–August. <a href="/references/enso-recurrence-2026-11-21.txt">Preserved source extracts and selected values</a>. See <a href="/methodology/#indices">ONI/RONI methods</a> before mixing products.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="analog-profiles-section" aria-labelledby="profiles-heading">
      <div class="section-header-compact"><span class="eyebrow">DATED OBSERVATIONS</span><h2 id="profiles-heading">What each benchmark actually shows</h2><p>These are the largest warm departures or coldest departure within the calendar year shown. They are weekly observations, not seasonal ONI or RONI strength classifications.</p></div>
      <div class="analog-cards-grid">
        {benchmarks.map(b => (
          <article class={`analog-card ${b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'el-nino-' : 'la-nina-'}${b.id}`}>
            <div class="analog-card-header">
              <div class={`analog-card-icon ${b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'warm' : 'cyan'}`}>{b.category === 'el-nino' ? <Flame size={22} /> : <Snowflake size={22} />}</div>
              <div><span class="event-tag">{b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'Warm-year comparison' : 'Cold-year comparison'}</span><h3>{b.id} calendar year</h3></div>
            </div>
            <div class="analog-metric-row">
              <div><span class="analog-metric-label">{b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'WEEKLY MAXIMUM' : 'WEEKLY MINIMUM'}</span><span class="analog-metric-val">{signed(b.extreme.anomaly)}</span></div>
              <div><span class="analog-metric-label">WEEK CENTERED</span><span class="analog-metric-sub">{b.extreme.date}</span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="analog-card-text">{b.description}</p>
            <div class="analog-card-footer"><span class="analog-badge">{b.weeks.length} weekly observations</span></div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="analog-table-section" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
      <div class="section-header-compact"><span class="eyebrow">SAME DATASET · SAME BASELINE</span><h2 id="comparison-heading">Weekly benchmark summary</h2><p>All four stored series were checked against the CPC source on November 12, 2026. Values are rounded to one decimal place in the source.</p></div>
      <div class="table-container" role="region" aria-label="Weekly benchmark comparison" tabindex="0"><table class="analog-summary-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Calendar year</th><th scope="col">Weekly extreme</th><th scope="col">Week centered</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th><th scope="col">Observations</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{benchmarks.map(b => (
          <tr><td><strong>{b.id}</strong></td><td><strong>{signed(b.extreme.anomaly)}</strong> {b.category === 'el-nino' ? 'maximum' : 'minimum'}</td><td>{b.extreme.date}</td><td>{b.weeks[0].date} to {b.weeks.at(-1)?.date}</td><td>{b.weeks.length}</td></tr>
        ))}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="figure-note">Source: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/data/indices/wksst9120.for" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NOAA CPC weekly SST and anomaly file</a>. A weekly extreme is not a three-month seasonal peak. The 2020 line excludes 2021–2023 and cannot describe the full multi-winter episode.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section historical-limits" aria-labelledby="analog-limits-heading">
      <h2 id="analog-limits-heading">Does a stronger El Niño mean a wetter or snowier winter?</h2>
      <p>A larger Niño 3.4 anomaly describes a bigger ocean-temperature departure in that region. It does not specify rainfall, snowfall or storm counts at your location. Weekly conventional anomalies here also differ from the relative, three-month RONI used in CPC strength assessments.</p>
      <p>Historical analogs put the timing and size of an ocean signal in context. They are a small selected sample, not a probability forecast or a ranking of local damage. Other ocean patterns and atmospheric variability affect seasonal outcomes; individual storms and snow levels depend on shorter-range weather.</p>
      <p>For a North American winter question, start with the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated U.S. winter outlook</a>, then the <a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">California rain versus mountain snow</a> or <a href="/impacts/pacific-northwest-winter/">Pacific Northwest winter</a> guide. For other regions, see the <a href="/impacts/">regional impacts overview</a>. Use official local weather forecasts for travel and safety decisions.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Interpretation reference: <a href="/references/cpc-november-2026.txt">preserved November 12, 2026 CPC issue extracts and values</a>, checked November 13. This preserved record documents the index thresholds and dated probabilities, rather than a ranking of regional impacts. For seasonal circulation and geographic limits, see <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mhx/ensowhat">NWS ENSO background</a>. <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml">Current rolling CPC discussion</a>. <a href="/forecast/#super-el-nino-heading">Compare weekly anomalies and seasonal strength</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="source-callout"><Clock size={20} /><div><strong>What does CPC expect next?</strong><p>The official phase probabilities are a monthly expert assessment informed by observations and models. They are separate from these historical curves.</p></div><a href="/forecast/">Official forecast <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div>
  </div>
  <script>
    import { initCharts } from '../scripts/charts';
    initCharts();
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  #same-season-heading, #enso-frequency-heading, #event-years-heading { scroll-margin-top: 100px; }
  .seasonal-example { text-align: left; }
  .seasonal-example caption { padding: 14px; }
  .historical-limits p { margin-top: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--muted); }
  .figure-note { display: block; }
  .analog-summary-table { min-width: 780px; }
</style>
